## CONFIGURATION AND SETUP

### 16. What hardware requirements must be met before installing an FCI?

1. All nodes must have similar hardware configuration with identical operating system version and patch levels
2. Identical SQL Server version, patch level, and installed components required for full FCI functionality
3. All nodes require network connectivity with sufficient bandwidth for heartbeat communication and health monitoring
4. Each node requires dedicated IP address for cluster communication plus participation in virtual network name IPs
5. Shared storage must be accessible simultaneously by all nodes through SAN, iSCSI, Storage Spaces Direct, or networks
6. All nodes must have identical SQL Server instance names and service account configuration

---

### 17. Can SQL Server FCIs be installed on virtual machines?

1. Yes, SQL Server FCIs are supported on Hyper-V virtual machines allowing maximum hardware utilization
2. Virtual machines must be configured with sufficient resources to handle failover scenarios effectively
3. Network configuration requires separate virtual network adapters for cluster communication and client connections
4. Pass-through disk attachment or live migration must be configured to enable automated failover between hosts
5. Virtual machine snapshots and checkpoints simplify disaster recovery but should not replace backup strategies
6. Hypervisor-level high availability should be configured alongside SQL Server clustering for maximum availability

---

### 18. What operating systems support SQL Server FCIs?

1. Supported operating systems include Windows Server 2012, 2012 R2, 2016, 2019, and 2022 versions
2. Windows Server Datacenter editions required for advanced features like multi-subnet clustering and Storage Spaces Direct
3. Each Windows Server version has specific requirements for cluster validation, hotfixes, and configuration settings
4. SQL Server editions must match Windows Server capabilities for clustering support and feature availability
5. Before installation verify operating system version and patch level meet Microsoft's recommended configuration
6. Unsupported OS combinations may result in unsupported cluster configurations ineligible for Microsoft support

---

### 19. What is cluster validation and why is it essential?

1. Cluster validation is comprehensive testing confirming all nodes, hardware, storage, and network meet Microsoft support requirements
2. Validation wizard included in Failover Cluster Manager guides through series of tests including network, storage, MSDTC, DNS
3. All tests must pass with green checkmarks or yellow triangles; red Xs indicate problems requiring resolution
4. Validation confirms configuration is supported and behaves predictably under failure conditions
5. Skipping validation risks unsupported configuration that behaves unpredictably or becomes ineligible for support
6. Re-run validation after any significant hardware changes or configuration updates to maintain support status

---

### 20. What is the MSDTC resource in an FCI?

1. MSDTC (Microsoft Distributed Transaction Coordinator) resource enables distributed transactions spanning multiple databases
2. Windows Server 2008 or later allows multiple MSDTC resources on single failover cluster with first becoming default
3. If no MSDTC resource exists, SQL Server uses MSDTC service running locally on each node
4. Best practice creates dedicated clustered MSDTC resource for FCIs using distributed transactions
5. MSDTC configuration managed through Component Services Management Console (dcomcnfg)
6. SQL Server 2008 and later support configuring MSDTC resources during setup or after FCI installation

---

### 21. Must SQL Server binaries be installed identically on all nodes?

1. Yes, SQL Server binaries must be installed identically including version, patch level, components, and instance name
2. Binaries installed locally on each node (not shared storage) allowing individual node management
3. Installation should be performed in same order with identical options across all nodes for consistency
4. Service packs and cumulative updates must be applied identically to maintain configuration consistency
5. New active node must have identical binaries during failover ensuring proper startup and feature functionality
6. Regular patching requires coordinated approach applying updates to passive nodes first before active node

---

### 22. How should SQL Server login accounts be configured in an FCI?

1. SQL Server login accounts must be identical across all FCI nodes including domain accounts for all services
2. Domain accounts must have identical rights and permissions on all nodes ensuring cross-node functionality
3. Local Windows accounts should not be used in FCIs because local account identity differs between nodes
4. Security configuration should use domain accounts with minimal required permissions following principle of least privilege
5. Test account functionality after failover ensuring jobs and services start properly on new active node
6. Managed service accounts can automatically manage password changes across all cluster nodes

---

## FAILOVER MECHANISMS

### 23. What are the types of failovers that can occur in an FCI?

1. Automatic failover occurs when WSFC cluster detects failure meeting configured failover policy conditions
2. Automatic failover triggered by SQL Server service stopped, repeated login failures, or memory pressure conditions
3. Cluster automatically transfers resource group to healthy node without administrator intervention during automatic failover
4. Manual failover occurs when administrators explicitly request resource group transfer using Failover Cluster Manager
5. Manual failover used for maintenance, testing, or planned upgrades to specific nodes
6. Both failover types transparent to client applications connected through virtual network name

---

### 24. What happens during automatic failover of an FCI?

1. WSFC detects failure through heartbeat communication and health checks indicating active node is unresponsive
2. All dirty pages in buffer cache written to disk unless hardware failure prevents disk access
3. All SQL Server services on active node stopped by WSFC orchestration
4. Resource group ownership transferred to healthy node selected by WSFC based on preferences
5. New owner starts SQL Server services bringing instance online; virtual name registered to new node through DNS
6. Client applications receive network errors, reconnect through virtual name resolving to new node IP address

---

### 25. What triggers automatic failover in an FCI?

1. Hardware failures including power loss, network card failure, or SCSI controller failure trigger failover
2. Operating system failures like kernel panic or unexpected shutdown trigger automatic failover
3. Application or service failures detected through health monitoring trigger failover if configured
4. Repeated login failures indicating instance not accepting connections trigger automatic failover
5. SQL Server service termination or unexpected shutdown triggers failover
6. Memory pressure or repeated out-of-memory errors trigger failover in some configurations

---

### 26. What is the failover policy and how is it configured?

1. Failover policy is set of rules determining conditions triggering automatic failover and controlling failure response
2. Flexible failover policy in SQL Server 2012 and later allows granular configuration at SQL Server component level
3. Components monitored include Database Engine, SQL Server Agent, and other services with configurable thresholds
4. Health detection uses dedicated connections polling active SQL Server for detailed component diagnostics
5. Failover policy configured through SQL Server Management Studio Always On Dashboard or Windows PowerShell
6. Detailed component diagnostics logged enabling better troubleshooting after failover by identifying trigger component

---

### 27. What is connection failover and how is it handled?

1. Connection failover occurs when client connections interrupted during failover to new active node
2. Existing connections receive network errors as active node becomes unavailable
3. Applications must reconnect through same virtual network name now resolving to new active node
4. Connection retry logic in client drivers and application code automatically attempts reconnection
5. SQL Server client libraries support transparent connection failover with configurable retry logic
6. New connections immediately accepted on new active node as SQL Server services start and name is registered

---

### 28. What is indirect checkpoint and how does it help failover?

1. Indirect checkpoints in SQL Server 2012 and later throttle dirty pages kept in buffer cache during normal operations
2. Proactively writes dirty pages to disk maintaining target recovery work rather than waiting for user-initiated checkpoint
3. Significantly fewer dirty pages remain in cache during failover reducing disk write time before database comes online
4. Makes recovery time objective (RTO) more predictable because disk write time during failover is reduced
5. Increases I/O load during normal operations but makes failover faster and more reliable
6. Organizations with strict RTO requirements should enable and tune indirect checkpoints based on I/O overhead tolerance

---

### 29. Can failover be tested without causing downtime?

1. No, testing automatic failover in production FCI temporarily interrupts client connections and services
2. Minimize disruption by testing during planned maintenance windows or on non-critical production systems
3. Manual failover can be initiated through Failover Cluster Manager deliberately transferring resource group
4. Testing validates failover occurs within acceptable time windows, client reconnection works properly
5. Regular failover testing essential to validate high availability mechanisms function before actual failure
6. After testing, failover back to original node if desired to maintain preferred node arrangement

---

### 30. What happens to running transactions during failover?

1. All running transactions on active node are terminated during failover
2. Client applications receive connection errors indicating connection was lost
3. Transactions not replicated to passive nodes because nodes share identical database files
4. Database consistency maintained as database brought online from last successful checkpoint
5. Uncommitted transactions rolled back ensuring database integrity
6. Applications must be designed to handle transaction failures and retry failed operations after reconnection

---

## QUORUM MODES AND VOTING

### 31. What are the quorum modes supported by WSFC?

1. Node Majority mode requires more than half of voting nodes to be online and communicating
2. Node and Disk Majority mode requires majority of nodes or quorum disk plus majority of online nodes
3. Node and File Share Majority mode uses file share witness instead of disk for geographic flexibility
4. Cloud Witness mode uses Microsoft Azure storage as witness eliminating local infrastructure requirements
5. Each mode provides different fault tolerance levels; choice depends on cluster topology and requirements
6. Node Majority recommended for odd-numbered clusters; other modes provide options for even-numbered clusters

---

### 32. What is Node Majority quorum mode?

1. Node Majority requires more than half of voting nodes in cluster to be online voting affirmatively
2. In three-node cluster, two nodes must be online; in five-node cluster, three nodes required
3. Recommended as default for odd-numbered clusters; simple and straightforward quorum determination
4. If majority of nodes offline, cluster automatically shuts down preventing split-brain scenarios
5. No additional witness infrastructure required reducing complexity and infrastructure cost
6. Default recommendation for most FCI deployments due to simplicity and ease of troubleshooting

---

### 33. What is Node and Disk Majority quorum mode?

1. Requires either majority of voting nodes plus quorum disk, or quorum disk and majority of remaining nodes
2. Extends fault tolerance by using shared disk as tiebreaker allowing even-numbered clusters to tolerate single failure
3. Quorum disk must be continuously accessible by all nodes through shared storage
4. Recommended for even-numbered node clusters like two-node or four-node configurations
5. If quorum disk becomes unavailable, quorum may be lost requiring careful disk management
6. Quorum disk must be sized appropriately and monitored continuously for availability

---

### 34. What is Node and File Share Majority quorum mode?

1. Similar to Node and Disk Majority but uses file share as witness instead of physical disk
2. Witness file share contains cluster state information and votes on quorum decisions
3. File share can be located on standalone server or network-attached storage accessible from all nodes
4. Useful when shared disk storage unavailable or impractical
5. File share witness less expensive than dedicated disk storage but requires reliable network connectivity
6. Commonly used for branch office or stretched cluster deployments requiring independent witness location

---

### 35. What is a Cloud Witness in WSFC?

1. Cloud Witness in Windows Server 2016 and later uses Microsoft Azure blob storage as witness
2. Eliminates need for physical witness infrastructure simplifying deployment for cloud-integrated organizations
3. Cluster nodes authenticate to Azure storage account to participate in quorum voting
4. Provides similar functionality to file share witness with improved accessibility from geographically distributed clusters
5. Excellent choice for multi-site or stretched clusters requiring high availability without physical witness maintenance
6. Requires Azure subscription and network connectivity to Azure endpoints for witness communication

---

### 36. What is dynamic quorum in WSFC?

1. Dynamic quorum in Windows Server 2012 R2 and later automatically adjusts required votes based on node availability
2. If nodes go offline unexpectedly, dynamic quorum automatically reduces quorum requirement for remaining nodes
3. Removes votes from offline nodes; after extended period offline node votes can be permanently removed
4. Significantly improves cluster availability during phased failures or rolling updates
5. Requires understanding how quorum voting changes over time during maintenance activities
6. May need manual adjustment if permanent node removal occurs

---

### 37. What is dynamic witness in WSFC?

1. Dynamic witness automatically manages witness disk or file share voting participation based on cluster state
2. If cluster has odd number of voting nodes without witness, witness vote dynamically removed
3. If cluster has even number of nodes, witness vote dynamically added
4. Automatic adjustment simplifies cluster configuration reducing misconfiguration risk
5. Requires Windows Server 2012 R2 or later and SQL Server 2012 R2 or later support
6. Works seamlessly with dynamic quorum feature for optimal quorum stability

---

### 38. How does nodweight affect quorum voting?

1. NodeWeight is per-node setting determining whether each node participates in quorum voting
2. Each node assigned NodeWeight of 1 (participates) or 0 (does not participate in voting)
3. Nodes with NodeWeight 0 remain cluster members but do not count toward quorum voting
4. Useful for read-only or non-SQL Server nodes preventing them from affecting quorum calculations
5. NodeWeight modifiable after cluster creation using Failover Cluster Manager or PowerShell
6. Total node weights plus witness weights should be odd ensuring clear quorum decisions

---

### 39. What is quorum failure and what are its impacts?

1. Quorum failure occurs when healthy voting nodes no longer satisfy quorum model for cluster
2. Happens if more than half nodes offline, witness disk unavailable, or network partitioning isolates minority
3. WSFC cluster automatically goes offline as safety measure preventing split-brain scenarios
4. All SQL Server instances and services associated with cluster are stopped
5. All client connections to FCI lost and databases become unavailable
6. Manual intervention required using forced quorum procedures to restore cluster operation

---

### 40. How do you recover from quorum failure?

1. Identify which nodes online and available; examine Windows Event logs and SQL Server error logs
2. On healthiest surviving node, manually force cluster to start without quorum using forced quorum procedure
3. WSFC service starts with forced quorum flag temporarily overriding quorum voting checks
4. Normally start WSFC service on each other healthy node one at time preventing race conditions
5. Apply new quorum mode and voting configuration reflecting surviving cluster topology
6. Verify healthy quorum re-established; recover availability group databases as needed

